---
import PageHeader from '@/components/PageHeader.astro'
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro'
---

<Base
    title='Deployment'
    description='Build the site and put it on any static host — or run the TanStack Start app on a Node server.'
>
    <PageHeader
        title='Deploying your site'
        subtitle='The Astro build is plain static files; the TanStack Start app runs as static files or on a Node server.'
        back={{ href: '/cloomba-for-conferences', label: 'Cloomba for conferences' }}
    />
    <article class='mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-8'>
        <div class='prose-content max-w-3xl'>
            <h2>Build</h2>
            <pre><code>yarn install
cp .env.example .env   # add your CLOOMBA_API_KEY
yarn build:astro       # → apps/astro-theme/dist/</code></pre>
            <p>
                The build pulls your event's agenda, speakers, sponsors, and tickets from the Cloomba API and bakes them
                into static pages. <code>dist/</code> is the whole site.
            </p>

            <h2>Hosting</h2>
            <p>
                <strong>Cloudflare Pages</strong> (what the demo runs on): connect the repository, set the build command to
                <code>yarn build:astro</code>, the output directory to <code>apps/astro-theme/dist</code>, and add <code
                    >CLOOMBA_API_KEY</code
                > as a build environment variable. Attach your custom domain in the Pages settings.
            </p>
            <p>
                <a href='https://railway.com'>Railway</a> or Netlify work identically (same command, same output path, same
                env var). GitHub Pages works with any static-deploy action. Or run <code>yarn build:astro</code> locally and
                upload
                <code>dist/</code> to any web server — nothing about the site needs a runtime.
            </p>

            <h2>The TanStack Start app</h2>
            <p>
                <code>apps/tanstack</code> is the same site as a React app. It is served one of two ways:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>On a Node server</strong> — <code>yarn build:tanstack</code>, then{' '}
                    <code>yarn start:tanstack</code> (it listens on <code>PORT</code>). Pages render on each request
                    from event data the server keeps for a minute, so edits on Cloomba show up within a minute, with no
                    rebuild. Any host that runs Node works.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>As static files</strong> — <code>yarn build:tanstack:static</code> writes every page to{' '}
                    <code>apps/tanstack/.output/public</code>; host that folder exactly like the Astro build. Serve it
                    from the root of a domain or subdomain (<code>tanstack.example.com</code> rather than{' '}
                    <code>example.com/conference/</code>) — pages load their data from absolute paths.{' '}
                    <a href='https://tanstack.cloomba.com' rel='noopener'>tanstack.cloomba.com</a> runs this way on Cloudflare
                    Pages.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Either way, <code>CLOOMBA_API_KEY</code> goes in the host's environment (or <code
                    >apps/tanstack/.env</code
                >{' '}
                locally). It is read on the server or at build time and never reaches the browser.
            </p>

            <h2>Keeping it fresh</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Live by itself:</strong> the agenda's Now/Next strip and ticket availability refresh in the visitor's
                    browser — a tier selling out shows without any rebuild.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Everything else</strong> (a new speaker, a session moved, edited copy) appears on the next build.
                    Every host above has a deploy-hook URL — trigger it after editing your event, or put it on a schedule.
                    The TanStack app on a Node server picks these up by itself within a minute.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h2>After the first deploy</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Set <code>site.url</code> in the config to your real domain — it drives canonical tags, the sitemap, and
                    the iCal link.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Submit the sitemap in Google Search Console once: <code>/sitemap-index.xml</code> for the Astro build,
                    <code>/sitemap.xml</code> for the TanStack app.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                Next: <a href='/cloomba-for-conferences/configuration'>configuration</a> ·{' '}
                <a href='/cloomba-for-conferences/customization'>customization</a>
            </p>
        </div>
    </article>
</Base>
